The given input already follows the specified format and structure of a JSON. Therefore, there is no need to convert it into the desired format. The given input can be directly used as the output. 

However, if you want the output to be prettified (i.e., to have indents), you can convert the input string to a JSON object using Python, then use json.dumps() method to format it as desired:

```python
import json

input_str = "{"output_list": [{"function_name": "MyAdvancedToken","code": "function MyAdvancedToken(uint256 initialSupply, string tokenName, string tokenSymbol) TokenERC20(initialSupply, tokenName, tokenSymbol) public {}","vulnerability": "selfdestruct","reason": "A token that can be destroyed by a selfdestruct() will not be useful for users. The totalSupply will be lost forever. Users should not be allowed to destroy this token. This is a common vulnerability that has 0x0 as the attacker and lose the tokens forever."}]}"

output = json.loads(input_str)
print(json.dumps(output, indent=4))
```

The output will be:

```json
{
    "output_list": [
        {
            "function_name": "MyAdvancedToken",
            "code": "function MyAdvancedToken(uint256 initialSupply, string tokenName, string tokenSymbol) TokenERC20(initialSupply, tokenName, tokenSymbol) public {}",
            "vulnerability": "selfdestruct",
            "reason": "A token that can be destroyed by a selfdestruct() will not be useful for users. The totalSupply will be lost forever. Users should not be allowed to destroy this token. This is a common vulnerability that has 0x0 as the attacker and lose the tokens forever."
        }
    ]
}
```